  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Corecivic’s FY2026 to date federal business shows continuity in scale and buyer concentration. Corecivic has $399M in contract obligations, spread across 41 awards, with an average award size of $9.7M. Obligations are up 0.9% year over year. The Department of Homeland Security is the largest buyer, obligating $318M, followed by the Department of Justice with $81.7M. Security Guards and Patrol Services is the largest NAICS market for Corecivic at $336M, with Other Justice, Public Order, and Safety Activities at $33.3M and Other Residential Care Facilities at $30.5M. Yearly obligations show a steady range: FY21 $272M, FY22 $288M, FY23 $253M, FY24 $271M, FY25 $396M, and FY26 $399M. Current-year totals are partial because figures are fiscal year to date. This profile points to a portfolio concentrated in DHS work and security services, with most spending occurring through a core set of large awards rather than a broad mix of smaller purchases.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Corecivic's federal business trended year over year?",
        "body": "Corecivic's obligations are up 0.9% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations have ranged from $253M to $399M across FY21 to FY26, with FY25 at $396M and FY26 at $399M. The current-year total is a fiscal year to date figure, so the FY26 number is partial. The pattern shows repeated large-year totals rather than a long-term decline or single spike."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Corecivic's main agency buyers and what markets do they buy?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security is Corecivic's top buyer with $318M in obligations, followed by the Department of Justice with $81.7M. Security Guards and Patrol Services is the dominant market at $336M, then Other Justice, Public Order, and Safety Activities at $33.3M, and Other Residential Care Facilities at $30.5M. This mix reflects concentrated spending on security and justice-related services."
      }
    ]
  },
